Docker Model Runner (DMR) is a Docker feature that allows you to run AI models (LLMs) locally on your computer using Docker, without needing to rely on cloud AI APIs such as OpenAI, Anthropic, or Gemini.

Think of it as Docker Engine, but for managing and running local AI models.

For example, you can run models such as Llama, Gemma, or Qwen locally and interact with them through an API.

The architecture is relatively simple:
1. Docker Model Runner: Manages the AI model runtime.
2. AI Model: The actual LLM, such as Llama or Qwen.
3. API: Exposes an endpoint that applications can call.
4. Your application: Sends prompts and receives AI-generated responses.
Your application can communicate with the model through an OpenAI-compatible API.


docker hub for ai : https://hub.docker.com/u/ai



If Docker Model Runner is enabled in Docker Desktop, you can use PowerShell:

# Check Model Runner status
docker model status

# Download a model
docker model pull ai/qwen3

# List downloaded models
docker model ls

# Run the model
docker model run ai/qwen3

docker model rm .. 




You can then chat with the model directly in your terminal.

You don't need to create a Dockerfile or manually configure a container for every model. Docker manages the model and its inference runtime


3. Why is this useful?
Benefit	Explanation
Privacy	Prompts can stay on your machine
Cost	No per-token cloud API charges
Offline use	You can use downloaded models without internet
Integration	Connect your applications through an API
Development	Test AI applications without a cloud provider

4. Docker Model Runner vs Ollama
These solve similar problems.
Feature	Docker Model Runner	Ollama
Run LLMs locally	Yes	Yes
Model management	Docker CLI	Ollama CLI
OpenAI-compatible API	Yes	Yes
Docker ecosystem integration	Native	External integration
Multiple inference engines	Yes	Primarily Ollama runtime